{{also|tènement}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|xno}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|tenement}}, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{m|la|tenementum}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} verb {{m|la|teneo}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[building]] that is [[rented]] to [[multiple]] [[tenant]]s, especially a low-rent, [[run-down]] one.
# {{lb|en|legal}} Any form of [[property]] that is held by one person from another, rather than being owned.
#: ''The island of Brecqhou is a '''tenement''' of Sark.''
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Dwelling; abode; habitation.
#* John Locke
#*: Who has informed us that a rational soul can inhabit no '''tenement''', unless it has just such a sort of frontispiece?

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|building}} {{l|en|tenement house}}, {{l|en|apartment building}}

